.SS "int32_t schultz_dash_register (\fBschultz_resource_table\fP * table, const float * lengths, uint32_t count, \fBschultz_handle\fP * out_handle)"

.PP
Registers a dash pattern\&. The lengths alternate between drawn and skipped, starting with drawn, and repeat for as long as the line does\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItable\fP The table to register with\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fIlengths\fP The lengths, in pixels\&. All must be positive\&. 
.br
\fIcount\fP How many, from 2 to SCHULTZ_DASH_MAX\&. 
.br
\fIout_handle\fP Receives the handle\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_ARGUMENT, or SCHULTZ_ERR_OUT_OF_MEMORY\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_dash_pair (\fBschultz_resource_table\fP * table, float on, float off, \fBschultz_handle\fP * out_handle)"

.PP
Registers the common dash pattern: one mark and one gap\&. The same thing as schultz_dash_register with a two length array, without the array\&. A pattern of more than two lengths, such as a dash dot rule, still goes through schultz_dash_register\&.
.PP
How far along the pattern a line starts is not set here\&. That is \fCdash_offset\fP on the stroke, so one registered pattern serves every offset\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItable\fP The table to register with\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fIon\fP How long each mark is, in pixels\&. Must be positive\&. 
.br
\fIoff\fP How long each gap is, in pixels\&. Must be positive\&. 
.br
\fIout_handle\fP Receives the handle\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_ARGUMENT when either length is not positive, or SCHULTZ_ERR_OUT_OF_MEMORY\&. 
.RE
.PP

